      "message": "test: avoid BSD ephemeral-port collisions in HTTP clients\n\nFreeBSD and OpenBSD allocate ephemeral ports from ranges that overlap\nthe functional framework's fixed test ports. An outgoing localhost\nconnection can therefore select its destination port as its source port\nand connect to itself. The wrong-RPC-port startup test exposed this on\nFreeBSD as BadStatusLine: POST / HTTP/1.1, consistent with reading its\nown request instead of receiving the expected connection refusal.\n\nThe existing set_ephemeral_port_range() protection only covers\nexplicitly created sockets.\n\nPython's HTTP clients create their sockets internally, so apply the\nhelper through their connection factory before connecting. Share the\nprotected HTTPConnection across the RPC proxy and direct HTTP callers,\nwithout changing system-wide settings. The HTTPS path is left alone\nbecause bitcoind does not serve RPC over TLS.\n\nAlso adds a framework unit test checking that the option is set before\nconnect() for IPv4 and IPv6 on both platforms.",
